In the beginning, there were two villages separated by a vast river. Each village had only what grew on its own shore - one had grain, the other had fruit.

One day, a villager from the grain shore built a raft and sent five sacks across the river. But the river was magical: whatever crossed its waters multiplied three-fold. The five sacks became fifteen.

The fruit villagers rejoiced at this abundance. They faced a choice: keep all fifteen sacks, or return some to honor the gift? The elders debated. Some said, "We owe nothing - the river's magic is not our doing." Others said, "Without their initial trust, we would have nothing."

They decided to return seven sacks. When these crossed back, they too multiplied, and the grain village prospered.

